  "mechanisms": [
    "Light-induced effects on human physiology and cognition are primarily mediated by intrinsically photosensitive retinal ganglion cells (ipRGCs) containing melanopsin, which are sensitive to blue wavelengths (460-480 nm). These non-visual effects influence arousal, mood, alertness, and learning/memory by affecting related brain areas.",
    "Arousal-level theories suggest an optimal illuminance level for task performance, where high light stimulation might lead to better performance on simple tasks but poorer performance on more complex tasks."
  ],
  "moderators": [
    "Task difficulty (mentioned in literature review as a factor influencing ILL effects, but not explicitly tested as a moderator in this study's findings)",
    "Time-of-day (suggested as a potential moderator for future research)",
    "Presence of other students (suggested as a potential moderator for future research)",
    "Participant viewpoint position (suggested as a potential moderator for future research)"
  ],
  "implementation_implications": [
    "The findings can be used by engineers, architects, lighting designers, and researchers to improve the energy efficiency of university campuses and establish lighting environments that enhance memory-related cognitive processes.",
    "Utilizing new LED-type technologies, energy consumption in classrooms can be considerably reduced by decreasing illuminance (e.g., to 100 lx) and modifying CCTs (e.g., to 6500 K) while simultaneously improving students' cognitive development."
  ],
  "domains": [
